course: dictionary
lesson: 14693
title: Prevalent: common and widespread
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Prevalent means common, or found in many places or at many times. When something is prevalent, you meet it a lot. This is a formal word, so you will often find it in science, health and geography lessons.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «Colds are prevalent in winter.» Colds happen a lot in winter, so prevalent must mean common in winter.
explain: Check by swapping in a simpler word. "Colds are common in winter" still makes sense, so common is a good meaning. If the swap does not make sense, look again at the clues.
explain: Watch for the phrases prevalent in, prevalent among and widely prevalent. They tell you where or who something is common, as in «Flu was prevalent among the children last month.»
fact: Prevalent is an adjective. Its forms are more prevalent and most prevalent.
fact: «Prevalent» means common or widespread in a place or at a time.
fact: In a formal way, «prevalent» can also mean the most common, or the strongest among others.
fact: «Prevalent among» tells you which group something is common in, for example «prevalent among young children».
fact: Common is a simple synonym for prevalent. Prevalent is more formal, so writers use it in science and health texts.
exercise: «Colds are prevalent in winter.» What does prevalent mean? | Very cold and icy | *Common or widespread | Rare and hard to find | Only seen by scientists
exercise: Mia says prevalent means unusual. Is she right? | Yes, it means rare | *No, it means common or widespread | No, it means hidden | Yes, it means dangerous
exercise: «Flu was prevalent among the children in the school last month.» Which group was flu common among? | Nobody at all | The teachers only | The dogs in the park | *The children in the school
test: «Rain is prevalent in this region, so the plants stay green all year.» What does prevalent mean? | *Common | Hidden | Empty | Frozen
test: Which word is the closest synonym for prevalent? | Gentle | Rare | Empty | *Common
exam: «Head lice were prevalent in the class this term.» What does prevalent mean here? | Dangerous and sudden | *Common or widespread | Only in the summer | Hard to see
